    body: `
<h2 id="table">The comparison</h2>
<div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl"><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Hard money</th><th>Conventional investor loan</th></tr>
<tr><td>Time to close</td><td>5–10 business days</td><td>30–45 days</td></tr><tr><td>Rate</td><td>8–13%</td><td>6.5–8% (investor pricing)</td></tr><tr><td>Points/fees</td><td>1–3 points + $1–2K</td><td>0–1 point + typical closing costs</td></tr><tr><td>Term</td><td>6–24 months, balloon</td><td>15–30 years, amortizing</td></tr><tr><td>Down payment</td><td>10–25% of cost</td><td>15–25% of price; no rehab funds</td></tr><tr><td>Property condition</td><td>Any; rehab financed</td><td>Must be habitable and appraise as-is</td></tr><tr><td>Income docs</td><td>None (liquidity only)</td><td>Full: tax returns, DTI ≤ 45%</td></tr><tr><td>Entity borrower</td><td>Required/preferred</td><td>Usually not allowed (Fannie/Freddie)</td></tr><tr><td>Financed property limit</td><td>None</td><td>10 financed properties (agency)</td></tr><tr><td>Prepayment penalty</td><td>Rare</td><td>None</td></tr></table></div>
<h2 id="when-hard">When hard money is the right tool</h2>
<ul><li>The property will not pass a conventional appraisal (no kitchen, roof damage, code violations).</li><li>The seller requires a 14-day close or you are buying at auction.</li><li>You need the rehab budget financed.</li><li>You own more than 10 financed properties, or your tax returns do not support the payment.</li><li>You are buying through an LLC and want the loan in the entity's name.</li></ul>
<h2 id="when-conv">When conventional is the right tool</h2>
<ul><li>The property is move-in ready and you intend to hold it for years.</li><li>You have 30+ days and W-2 or stable self-employment income.</li><li>You are under the financed-property limit and have 20–25% down.</li><li>The deal's return depends on the lowest possible payment, not on speed.</li></ul>
<h2 id="example">Worked example: a $300,000 flip</h2>
<p>Purchase $300,000, rehab $60,000, ARV $480,000, 6-month project. <strong>Hard money</strong> at 10% with 2 points on a $324,000 loan (90% LTC): $16,200 interest + $6,480 points + $2,000 fees = $24,680 financing cost, and you fund $36,000 of cost plus the points. <strong>Conventional</strong> is unavailable because the property is distressed and the rehab is not financed — you would need $120,000 cash for 20% down plus the full $60,000 rehab. The hard money deal nets roughly $480,000 − $360,000 cost − $24,680 financing − $38,000 selling costs ≈ $57,000 on about $45,000 of cash deployed. The financing cost is real, but the deal does not exist without it.</p>
<h2 id="both">Using both: the two-loan structure</h2>
<p>Most rental investors eventually run a hybrid: hard money to acquire and renovate, then a refinance into a 30-year <a href="/loans/rental-dscr/">DSCR loan</a> or, for borrowers with strong income and few properties, a conventional investor loan. The hard money loan's cost is then just the “admission price” to a property that could not be bought any other way. Read our <a href="/learn/brrrr-financing-with-hard-money/">BRRRR financing guide</a> for the sequencing.</p>`,
    faqs: [['Can I refinance a hard money loan into a conventional loan?', 'Yes, if you qualify on income and the property is finished. Expect a 6-month seasoning requirement for cash-out on the new value with agency loans; DSCR lenders are often more flexible.'], ['Is hard money ever cheaper than conventional?', 'Almost never on rate. It can be cheaper in total when it lets you buy at a deeper discount (cash-like offer) or avoid a lost deal.']],
